{{<shell}}{{$content}}
<h1>Machine</h1>
<p class="hint">What this box is doing, read from <code>/proc</code> where there is one. Anything it
  cannot read is left blank rather than guessed at &mdash; a made-up number on a page like this is
  worse than a missing one, because somebody acts on it.</p>

<h2>Processor</h2>
<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{#cpuKnown}}{{cpuNow}}%{{/cpuKnown}}{{^cpuKnown}}&mdash;{{/cpuKnown}}</div><div class="l">right now</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{cpuAverage}}</div><div class="l">last {{averageMinutes}} min</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{processors}}</div><div class="l">cores</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{load}}</div><div class="l">load average</div></div>
</div>
<p class="hint">Load average is <strong>runnable processes, not a percentage</strong> &mdash; that is
  {{loadPerCore}} per core here. The percentage above is what <code>top</code> computes: the share of
  the time since the last sample that was not idle.</p>
{{^cpuKnown}}<p class="hint">No <code>/proc/stat</code> on this machine, so there is no processor
  figure. Everything else on this page still works.</p>{{/cpuKnown}}

<h2>Memory</h2>
<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{heapPercent}}%</div><div class="l">JVM heap</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{heapUsed}}</div><div class="l">of {{heapMax}}</div></div>
  {{#hostKnown}}
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{hostPercent}}%</div><div class="l">host memory</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{hostAvailable}}</div><div class="l">available of {{hostTotal}}</div></div>
  {{/hostKnown}}
</div>
<p class="hint">Two numbers because they answer two questions. The <strong>heap</strong> says whether
  this program is under pressure; the <strong>host</strong> says whether the machine is, which is
  what decides whether the next thing to start on this box gets killed. Available rather than free,
  because free memory on Linux is nearly always small &mdash; the kernel uses the rest for cache, and
  reporting that would make every healthy machine look like it is falling over.</p>
{{^hostKnown}}<p class="hint">No <code>/proc/meminfo</code>, so the host numbers are not
  available.</p>{{/hostKnown}}

<h2>The last {{hours}} hours</h2>
{{^anyBars}}<p class="hint">Nothing sampled yet. A reading is taken every {{sampleMinutes}} minute(s),
  so this fills in as the server runs and starts again from empty after a restart &mdash; it is a
  window on the last day, not a record.</p>{{/anyBars}}
{{#anyBars}}
<div class="graph" role="img" aria-label="processor, host memory and heap over the last day">
  {{#bars}}<span class="col" title="{{when}} &middot; cpu {{cpu}}% &middot; host {{host}}% &middot; heap {{heap}}%">
    <b class="cpu" style="height:{{cpuHeight}}%"></b><b class="host" style="height:{{hostHeight}}%"></b><b class="heap" style="height:{{heapHeight}}%"></b>
  </span>{{/bars}}
</div>
<p class="hint"><span class="key cpu"></span> processor &nbsp; <span class="key host"></span> host
  memory &nbsp; <span class="key heap"></span> heap. {{samples}} sample(s) held, averaged into
  columns so a spike in a quiet hour still shows.</p>
{{/anyBars}}

<h2>This process</h2>
<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{hits}}</div><div class="l">requests</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{errorRate}}%</div><div class="l">errors</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{livePings}}</div><div class="l">live pings</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="n">{{eventsEmitted}}</div><div class="l">mutations</div></div>
</div>
<p class="hint">Up {{uptime}}, running {{version}}. Requests exclude the live channel, which is
  counted on its own &mdash; an open tab asks it every few seconds forever, and letting that into the
  numbers would push every page anybody reads off the bottom of them.
  <a href="{{eventsUrl}}">The mutation stream</a> keeps the last thousand row changes.</p>
{{/content}}{{/shell}}
